nuked-opl3 (LGPL-2.1-or-later)
------------------------------

The OPL3 core comes from `nuked-opl3`, a Rust port of Nuked-OPL3, and is
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as every derivative of Nuked-OPL3 is. It was chosen anyway
because it is the accurate one: it reproduces the chip register for register,
measured against silicon, where the permissively licensed alternatives
approximate the envelope and phase behavior. Nothing in the game's data
defines what the chip does with a register, so an approximation cannot be
checked against anything the project has.

The LGPL's condition on a work that links it statically is that whoever
receives the binary must be able to relink it against a modified version of the
library. For a Rust binary that means the object files or the sources have to
be on offer.

**This is discharged by source availability.** motionvm is distributed as
source; anyone with it can change the `nuked-opl3` dependency, rebuild, and get
a binary with their own version of the library in it. Nothing further is
required for that form of distribution.

A **pre-built binary** carries the obligation with it: alongside the binary
there has to be either its object files or the complete corresponding source,
so the recipient can relink. The project's own binary releases discharge it
that way: every release on GitHub carries, beside the Windows and macOS zips,
the source archive of the tagged commit they were built from, and
`cargo build --release` on that archive with a changed `nuked-opl3` is the
relink. Anyone else shipping a binary of motionvm takes the obligation on
themselves; it is the LGPL's requirement and not motionvm's.

The game data
-------------

The game's own files — artwork, text, music, script modules and `ENGINE.EXE` —
are **not** part of this project and are not redistributable. motionvm reads an
existing installation and ships none of it. See "Game data" in README.md.
